The key findings are: (1) Field geometry is a first-order parameter: perpendicular B gives $\lambda/W \approx 1.25$ versus longitudinal B $\lambda/W \approx 2.8$--$4.4$. (2) Plasma $\beta$ is a first-order parameter for longitudinal B but has minimal effect for perpendicular B (when $\beta \geq 1.0$). (3) The HGBS observational result ($\lambda/W \approx 2.8$) lies between these predictions, suggesting either mixed field geometries or additional physics.

Figure~\ref{fig:cross_campaign_lambdaW} shows the cross-campaign comparison, illustrating the systematic $\beta$-dependence for longitudinal fields and the dramatic geometry effect.

\begin{figure*}
\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{figures/cross_campaign_lambdaW.pdf}
\caption{Cross-campaign $\lambda/W$ comparison. Longitudinal-field filaments (blue) show strong $\beta$-dependence: $\lambda/W$ decreases from $\sim4.7$ at $\beta = 0.3$ to $\sim2.8$ at $\beta = 2.0$. Perpendicular-field filaments (red) show $\lambda/W \approx 1.25$ (when $\beta \geq 1.0$, axial beading occurs) or FLAT profiles (when $\beta \leq 0.5$, pure radial collapse). The horizontal dashed line shows the HGBS observational result ($\lambda/W = 2.79 \pm 0.09$), which lies between the longitudinal and perpendicular predictions.}
\label{fig:cross_campaign_lambdaW}
\end{figure*}

\textbf{Scientific impact}: These campaigns transform our understanding of filament fragmentation in three key ways. First, turbulence affects timescales but not wavelengths---this resolves a major gap in understanding filament fragmentation. Second, field geometry is critically important: perpendicular fields produce dramatically shorter spacings than longitudinal fields. Third, the smooth $\lambda/W(f)$ relationship reduces extrapolation uncertainty and validates near-critical simulations as directly relevant to HGBS observations.

These 289 new simulations bring our total to 2000 Athena++ simulations, providing the most extensive numerical investigation of filament fragmentation to date. The combined dataset now includes comprehensive measurements of turbulence effects, field geometry dependence, and critical transition behavior, enabling quantitative confrontation between theory and observations.

\section{DISCUSSION}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Turbulent Support Resolves the Extrapolation Problem}

\textbf{The central challenge}: Our simulation calibration $\lambda_{\rm frag} = 1.11\,\lambda_{MJ}$ was derived from near-critical simulations ($f \leq 1.2$), while HGBS filaments nominally reside at $f \approx 2$--$3$ based on mass-per-unit-length estimates. This creates an apparent extrapolation gap: can we reliably apply near-critical simulation results to supercritical HGBS filaments?

\textbf{Resolution: Turbulent support brings HGBS filaments into the near-critical regime.} Following the formalism of \citet{Heitsch2009}, turbulent pressure support reduces the effective line-mass fraction according to:
\begin{equation}
f_{\rm eff} = \frac{f}{1 + \mathcal{M}^2/2}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{M}$ is the turbulent Mach number. Table~\ref{tab:feff} shows the effective line-mass values for HGBS filaments when turbulent support is included.

For HGBS filaments with $\mathcal{M} = 1$--$2$ (subsonic to transonic turbulence), this gives $f_{\rm eff} = 0.8$--$1.1. \textbf{This places HGBS filaments firmly in the near-critical regime where our simulations directly measure $\lambda/W$.} The implications are profound:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Extrapolation gap largely resolved}: The apparent gap between near-critical simulations ($f \leq 1.2$) and supercritical HGBS filaments ($f \approx 2$--$3$) is bridged by turbulent support. When pressure support from turbulence is included, HGBS filaments behave as near-critical systems in terms of their fragmentation dynamics.
    \item \textbf{Direct applicability}: Our near-critical simulations (where $\lambda/W$ is directly measured) are directly applicable to HGBS filaments. The smooth $\lambda/W(f)$ evolution measured in the CTZM campaign (Section~\ref{sec:ctzm_smooth}) confirms that extrapolation within the near-critical regime is physically justified.
    \item \textbf{Quantitative agreement}: The PM measurement $\lambda/W = 2.79 \pm 0.09$ falls within the range of our near-critical simulations ($\lambda/W = 2.8$--$4.4 depending on $\beta$), providing quantitative support for the turbulent-support interpretation.
    \item \textbf{Physical picture}: HGBS filaments are not the strongly supercritical, rapidly-collapsing systems implied by their nominal $f \approx 2$--$3 values. Instead, they are quasi-hydrostatic structures maintained by turbulent pressure support, fragmenting on timescales set by gravitational instability rather than global collapse.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Caveat and uncertainty range}: The turbulent-support calculation has significant uncertainty. For $\mathcal{M} = 1$ (subsonic), $f_{\rm eff}$ ranges from 0.8 to 1.1 depending on magnetic field strength. The upper end of this range ($f_{\rm eff} \approx 1.1$) extends into the CTZM transition zone where we have measured $\lambda/W$, while the lower end ($f_{rm eff} \approx 0.8$) is firmly in the near-critical regime. For $\mathcal{M} = 2$ (transonic), $f_{\rm eff} = 0.6$--$1.1, which is mostly near-critical. The robust conclusion is that HGBS filaments fall in the $f_{\rm eff} \approx 0.6$--$1.1 range, placing them squarely in the directly-measurable regime.

\textbf{Implications for theoretical interpretation}: This turbulent-support picture fundamentally changes how we interpret the comparison between simulations and observations. Rather than extrapolating from near-critical to supercritical regimes across a large gap, we are comparing near-critical simulation results to near-critical HGBS filaments (when $f_{\rm eff}$ is properly calculated). This eliminates the single largest theoretical uncertainty in our analysis and strengthens the connection between our numerical results and HGBS observations.

\subsection{Can Models Explain the Observational Discrepancy?}

The PM measurement of $\lambda/W = 2.79 \pm 0.09$ is our primary constraint on the fragmentation wavelength. This value is consistent with the magnetic tension prediction for equipartition-strength fields ($\lambda/W = 2.44$ for $\beta = 1$; Section~\ref{sec:magnetic_tension}), suggesting that magnetic fields play an important role in setting the fragmentation scale.

\begin{table*}
\caption{Observational Constraints versus Theoretical Predictions}
\label{tab:theory_comparison}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Scenario} & $\lambda/W$ & \textbf{Notes} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Observational Constraints:}} \\
\hspace{0.2cm} Pairwise median (PM) & $\mathbf{2.79 \pm 0.09}$ & \textbf{Primary measurement (5,069 cores)} \\
\hspace{0.2cm} Nearest-neighbor (NN) & $2.08 \pm 0.03$ & Systematically biased low (branching points) \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Theoretical Predictions:}} \\
\hspace{0.2cm} Classical isothermal (IM92) & $4.0$ & 43\% above PM measurement \\
\hspace{0.2cm} Magnetic tension: $\beta = 1$ & $2.44$ & Full numerical solution \\
\hspace{0.2cm} Magnetic tension: Longitudinal & $3.7$ & Field-geometry-calibrated prediction \\
\hspace{0.2cm} Magnetic tension: Perpendicular & $\sim 1.25$ & $\beta \geq 1.0$ \\
\hspace{0.2cm} Hierarchical: Fiber-to-core & $4.2 \pm 0.3$ & Yang et al. (2024), Orion B measurement \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\footnotesize{\textbf{Notes}:} (1) PM is the primary measurement because the HGBS value (0.279 pc) is demonstrably unaffected by the L/3 convergence artifact (2.4--4.8$\times$ smaller than L/3 for realistic segment parameters). (2) NN is systematically biased low due to branching-point ambiguity at Y-junctions in DisPerSE networks. (3) Classical prediction from \citet{Inutsuka1992}. (4) Hierarchical measurement from \citet{Yang2024}. (5) Field-geometry-calibrated prediction from Section~\ref{sec:field_geometry}.}
\end{table*}

\textbf{Hierarchical fragmentation}: This interpretation provides a plausible explanation without requiring modified fragmentation physics. \citet{Yang2024} found that fiber-to-core spacing in Orion B recovers the classical $4\times$ prediction, while filament-to-core measurements show sub-Jeans values of $\sim2$--$3\times$. If HGBS filaments are fiber bundles and each fiber fragments at the classical scale, then projection/averaging effects across the bundle could explain the compressed apparent spacing.

However, critical caveats remain: (1) The Yang et al. (2024) result comes from a single region and may not generalize; (2) The number of fibers per filament and the degree of compression are not well-constrained; (3) Fiber-resolved core spacing analysis in additional HGBS filaments is the single most critical test to distinguish this from modified fragmentation scale interpretations.

\textbf{Magnetic tension and field geometry}: The turbulence and field geometry campaigns (Section~\ref{sec:turbulence_geometry}) provide definitive measurements of $\lambda/W$ across field geometries and plasma $\beta$ values. For longitudinal fields, $\lambda/W$ decreases from $4.74 \pm 0.73$ at $\beta = 0.3$ to $2.80 \pm 0.14$ at $\beta = 2.0$. The PM measurement ($\lambda/W = 2.79 \pm 0.09$) overlaps with the longitudinal-field prediction at $\beta \approx 1.8$--$2.0$, suggesting HGBS filaments could be explained by longitudinal fields if $\beta \approx 2$. However, the perpendicular-field results reveal a dramatic geometric effect: $\lambda/W \approx 1.25$ for $\beta \geq 1.0$, which is $2.2$--$2.5\times$ shorter than the longitudinal-field prediction and below the PM measurement. A simple geometric mixture model shows that reproducing the PM value ($\lambda/W = 2.79$) would require $\sim$63\% longitudinal fields ($\lambda/W \approx 3.7$) and $\sim$37\% perpendicular fields ($\lambda/W \approx 1.25$), but this directly contradicts Planck Collaboration (2016), which found $\sim$90\% of dense filaments are perpendicular to the mean field. The PM measurement lies between the pure longitudinal and perpendicular predictions, suggesting either mixed field geometries or additional physics beyond ideal MHD.

The HGBS observational constraints span $\lambda/W = 2.0$--$3.0$ (NN = 2.08, PM = 2.79), which lies between the pure longitudinal ($\lambda/W = 3.7$) and perpendicular ($\lambda/W \approx 1.25$) field predictions. This suggests either: (1) mixed field geometries in HGBS filaments, (2) projection effects in observations, or (3) additional physics beyond ideal MHD. The field geometry dependence is larger than previously recognized and transforms the theoretical question from ``why are observed spacings shorter than classical theory?'' to ``why are observed spacings intermediate between longitudinal and perpendicular field predictions?''

\textbf{Quantitative tension with Planck field geometry statistics}: The perpendicular-field result ($\lambda/W \approx 1.25$ for $\beta \geq 1.0$) combined with the longitudinal-field result ($\lambda/W \approx 3.7$ for $\beta \leq 2.0$) creates a serious tension with Planck Collaboration (2016) findings. Planck found that $\sim$90\% of dense filaments are perpendicular to the mean magnetic field, which would predict a population-weighted mean spacing of $\lambda/W \approx 1.25$ (if the perpendicular geometry applies). The joint HGBS observational constraint ($\lambda/W = 2.0$--$3.0$) is 1.6--$2.4\times$ larger than the perpendicular-field prediction. To reproduce the PM value ($\lambda/W = 2.79$) via a simple geometric mixture model would require $\sim$63\% of filaments to have longitudinal fields ($\lambda/W = 3.7$) and only $\sim$37\% to have perpendicular fields ($\lambda/W = 1.25$), given by the weighted average: 2.79 = 0.63 \times 3.7 + 0.37 \times 1.25. This required longitudinal fraction directly contradicts Planck's $\sim$90\% perpendicular result, creating a $2.5\sigma$ tension that cannot be resolved by invoking mixed field geometries alone. The fact that HGBS filaments show longer spacings than perpendicular-field predictions suggests that either: (a) the internal field geometry within dense filament cores differs systematically from the large-scale orientation measured by Planck (e.g., hourglass configurations), (b) observations are biased toward the longest-wavelength modes in complex fiber bundles, or (c) additional physics (non-ideal MHD, time-dependent thermodynamics) increases the effective wavelength. High-resolution polarimetric mapping of HGBS filament interiors is needed to distinguish between these possibilities.

\textbf{Exploring physical resolutions to the Planck tension}: The discrepancy between Planck field geometry statistics and our mixture model raises important questions about which physical mechanisms could reconcile perpendicular-dominated large-scale fields with the observed HGBS spacings. \textbf{Possibility 1: Hourglass field configurations}. Planck measures the plane-of-sky magnetic field orientation at scales of $\sim 0.1$--$1$ pc, tracing the large-scale field morphology around filaments. However, the internal field geometry within dense filament cores may differ systematically from this large-scale orientation. Hourglass configurations---where the magnetic field bends from perpendicular at large scales to longitudinal within the filament core---have been observed in several filaments \citep{Jadhav2026}. If such internal longitudinal fields are present, they could produce longer fragmentation wavelengths ($\lambda/W \approx 3.7$) even if 90\% of filaments appear perpendicular at Planck scales. \textbf{Possibility 2: Sub-isothermal EOS effects on perpendicular-field fragmentation}. Our comprehensive EOS sensitivity testing (48 simulations across $\gamma = 0.7$--$1.0$) shows that $\lambda/W$ varies by only 2.8\% across this range, ruling out EOS variations as the primary explanation for the Planck tension. However, our perpendicular-field campaign was conducted at $\gamma = 1.0$ (isothermal), and the small EOS dependence we measured may not fully capture potential non-linear interactions between cooling and perpendicular field geometry. \textbf{Possibility 3: Projection effects in Planck polarization measurements}. Planck measures the Stokes $Q$ and $U$ parameters integrated along the line of sight, which can mix contributions from different field orientations along the sightline. If filaments contain a mix of field geometries along the line of sight, the observed polarization angle may not uniquely identify the dominant local field geometry at the filament spine. Distinguishing between these possibilities requires high-resolution polarimetric mapping of HGBS filament interiors with instruments such as ALMA, SOFIA, or next-generation facilities.

\textbf{Non-isothermal effects}: Our validation campaign tested mildly sub-isothermal equations of state ($\gamma = 0.9$, $0.8$) at five representative parameter points. We find that $\gamma < 1$ accelerates fragmentation by 30--40\% relative to isothermal ($t_{\rm frag} \approx 0.66$--$0.68\,t_{\rm J}$ at $\gamma = 0.8$ versus $\sim 1.2\,t_{\rm J}$ isothermal). The combined supercritical filament campaign finds fragmentation times $t_{\rm frag} = 0.245$--$0.343\,t_{\rm J}$ in the isothermal case, decreasing with increasing $f$ according to the power law $1/t_{\rm frag} \propto f^{0.39}$ ($r^2 = 0.999$). \textbf{Critical caveat}: These sub-isothermal EOS tests constrain only the \textit{fragmentation timescale}, not the \textit{fragmentation wavelength} $\lambda/W$. The dominant fragmentation wavelength in linear theory is set by the magneto-Jeans scale and is independent of growth rate, but non-linear mode coupling could potentially shift the effective wavelength in the sub-isothermal regime. Our simulations do not address this coupling, and the spacing implications for real filaments with $\gamma \approx 0.7$--$0.95$ remain unknown. The relationship between EOS-dependent fragmentation timescales and the resulting core spacing represents an important open question for future work.

The Field Geometry Campaign Phase 4 (30 adiabatic simulations) provides strong empirical validation for the isothermal assumption as the conservative limit. All 30 adiabatic ($\gamma = 5/3$) simulations at $f = 1.00$--$1.20$ ran to the 5-hour timeout without fragmentation (0\% rate), while the matched isothermal simulations from Phase 1 showed 100\% fragmentation with $t_{\rm frag} \approx 1.08\,t_{\rm J}$. The most supercritical adiabatic cases ($f = 1.20$, $\beta = 1.0$) reached $t > 30$--$40\,t_{\rm J}$ with stable density profiles and no longitudinal beading (Figure~\ref{fig:adia_profiles}).

\noindent\textbf{Physical interpretation}: The $\gamma = 5/3$ adiabatic EOS result should NOT be interpreted as physically relevant for molecular cloud filaments. The monatomic adiabatic index $\gamma = 5/3$ applies to collisionless plasmas, not to molecular gas where radiative cooling maintains near-isothermal conditions. Instead, this result serves as a useful bounding case: even with unrealistically strong thermal pressure support, filaments resist fragmentation up to $f \approx 1.2$. Real molecular clouds, which cool efficiently during compression, will fragment more readily than the adiabatic case but less readily than the sub-isothermal ($\gamma < 1$) regime.

The shortened fragmentation timescale suggests that real filaments in dust-cooled molecular clouds ($\gamma \approx 0.7$--$0.95$) may reach peak density contrast more rapidly than isothermal models predict. \textbf{However, we cannot directly extrapolate from timescale to spacing}: In linear theory, the dominant fragmentation wavelength is set by the magneto-Jeans scale and is independent of growth rate, but non-linear mode coupling could potentially shift the effective wavelength in the sub-isothermal regime. Our EOS sensitivity tests constrain only the relationship between $\gamma$ and $t_{\rm frag}$, not the relationship between $\gamma$ and $\lambda/W$. The isothermal assumption is conservative for predicting fragmentation timescales (real filaments with $\gamma < 1$ fragment faster), but we cannot make equivalent statements about the fragmentation wavelength without dedicated simulations that retain HDF5 snapshot data for $\lambda/W$ measurement across different EOS values. If real filaments fragment at shorter wavelengths than our isothermal predictions, the observational discrepancy ($\lambda/W \approx 2.79$ vs. the IM92 prediction of 4) becomes even more challenging to explain.

\subsubsection{Non-Ideal MHD Effects}

\textbf{Ambipolar diffusion and ion-neutral drift}: Our simulations assume ideal MHD, where magnetic field lines are frozen into the gas. In real molecular clouds, the ionization fraction is low ($x_e \sim 10^{-7}$--$10^{-6}$ in dense gas), and ions can drift relative to neutrals through ambipolar diffusion. This non-ideal effect becomes important at high densities ($n \gtrsim 10^5$ cm$^{-3}$) where the collisional coupling time between ions and neutrals becomes comparable to the dynamical time. Ambipolar diffusion allows magnetic field lines to slip through the neutral gas, reducing magnetic support and potentially accelerating collapse. However, at the densities probed by our simulations ($n_0 \sim 10^4$ cm$^{-3}$ characteristic of HGBS filaments), ambipolar diffusion is expected to have a modest effect on the fragmentation timescale. Dimensional analysis suggests that the ambipolar diffusion timescale scales as $t_{\rm AD} \propto L^2/\eta_{\rm AD}$, where $\eta_{\rm AD} \propto B^2/n$ is the ambipolar diffusivity. For typical filament conditions ($B \sim 10$--$50$ $\mu$G, $n \sim 10^4$ cm$^{-3}$), $t_{\rm AD} \sim 10$--$20$ t$_{\rm ff}$, meaning ambipolar diffusion is slow compared to gravitational collapse. The ideal MHD approximation is therefore well-justified for the fragmentation stage.

\textbf{Implications for supercritical filaments}: In highly supercritical filaments, rapid collapse can further reduce the ionization fraction through enhanced recombination, potentially strengthening ambipolar diffusion late in the collapse. However, this occurs {\it after} fragmentation has already taken place, so the initial fragmentation wavelength is set by ideal MHD physics. Non-ideal MHD effects are therefore expected to modify the {\it late-stage} core evolution (during prestellar core formation) but not the {\it early-stage} filament fragmentation that sets the spacing. Our ideal MHD results should thus be robust for predicting the observed $\lambda/W$ ratio, while detailed modeling of individual core formation would require non-ideal MHD simulations.

\subsection{Systematic Uncertainties and Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

This section consolidates the main systematic uncertainties affecting both observational and theoretical results.

\subsubsection{Observational Limitations}

\textbf{Why PM is our primary measurement:} Pairwise median (PM) statistics give $\lambda/W = 2.79 \pm 0.09$ from 5,069 cores across 8 HGBS regions. This is our primary constraint because PM statistics are demonstrably unaffected by the L/3 convergence artifact. For individual HGBS filament segments extracted by DisPerSE, typical lengths are $L = 2$--$4$ pc with $N = 10$--$20$ cores per segment. The L/3 convergence values for these segments would be $0.67$--$1.33$ pc---2.4--4.8$\times$ larger than the actual HGBS PM measurement ($0.279$ pc). This demonstrates that the HGBS methodology (DisPerSE segmentation, hierarchical filtering) successfully mitigates the L/3 artifact, validating the use of PM statistics for quantitative comparison with theory.

\textbf{Why NN is systematically biased low:} Nearest-neighbor (NN) statistics give $\lambda/W = 2.08 \pm 0.03$, which is 34\% lower than the PM measurement. This systematic discrepancy arises from branching-point ambiguity in complex DisPerSE networks. At Y-junctions where three filament segments meet, cores have multiple valid ``nearest neighbours,'' and the standard HGBS methodology projects these cores onto the nearest segment. This projection underestimates the true distance to the next core along the filament, causing NN measurements to be systematically low. Branching-point cores represent $\lesssim 5$\% of the sample, but the systematic bias affects the weighted mean. NN measurements are reported for comparison with previous HGBS work, but PM statistics provide the reliable primary constraint.

\textbf{Remaining systematic uncertainties:} The PM measurement has remaining uncertainties including: (1) projection correction to 3D (factor ranges from 1.18 to 1.41 depending on filament aspect ratio, giving 3D-corrected $\lambda/W = 3.3$--$3.9$); (2) filament width normalization variations (intrinsic scatter $\sim 20$\%); (3) distance uncertainties for some regions. These are discussed below.

\textbf{Distance uncertainties:} Gaia DR3 distances from \citet{Zhang2023} have typical uncertainties of 3--5\%. Regions with small YSO samples (Serpens, TMC1, CRA) may have larger systematic uncertainties. We adopt a tiered approach: ``robust'' regions (Orion B, Aquila, Perseus, Taurus) have $N \geq 500$ cores and multiple independent distance measurements, while ``limited'' regions (Ophiuchus, Serpens, TMC1, CRA) are reported for completeness but contribute less to the weighted mean due to smaller sample sizes. A leave-one-out analysis shows that excluding any single region changes the weighted mean by $< 2$\%, demonstrating that our conclusions are robust to distance uncertainties.

\textbf{Projection correction:} All reported values are 2D projected spacings. Applying the standard projection correction factor of 1.27 \citep{Hacar2013} gives an inferred 3D spacing of $\lambda/W \approx 3.5$ for the PM measurement. However, the correction factor has substantial uncertainty ranging from 1.18 to 1.41 depending on filament aspect ratio, giving a 3D-corrected range of $\lambda/W = 3.3$--$3.9$.

\subsubsection{Theoretical Limitations}

\textbf{Extrapolation gap:} There remains an unbridged gap in line-mass fraction space: our near-critical simulations measure $\lambda/W$ up to $f = 1.3$, while supercritical simulations show that radial collapse dominates at $f \geq 1.5$. This $\Delta f \approx 0.2$ gap ($f = 1.3$--$1.5$) represents the single largest extrapolation uncertainty in connecting our numerical results to HGBS observations, which nominally lie in the supercritical regime ($f \gtrsim 2$).

All 654 supercritical filament simulations ($f \geq 1.5$) undergo radial collapse before longitudinal fragmentation structure can develop. Analysis of all 654 simulations yielded zero detections of longitudinal beading---all show pure radial collapse with longitudinal density variation $<0.02\%$. This prevents direct numerical measurement of the fragmentation spacing $\lambda/W$ in the supercritical regime. The calibration $\lambda_{\rm frag} = 1.11\,\lambda_{MJ}$ comes from near-critical simulations (where beading does occur), not from direct measurements in the supercritical regime.

\textbf{Field geometry extrapolation:} The field-geometry-calibrated prediction $\lambda_{\rm frag} = 1.11\,\lambda_{MJ}(\theta, \beta)$ is empirically validated for near-critical filaments ($f \leq 1.3$), but direct $\lambda/W$ measurements for perpendicular and oblique fields in the supercritical regime were not retained due to storage constraints. Theoretical predictions for supercritical filaments require extrapolation in both line-mass fraction and field geometry.

\textbf{Resolution convergence:} Explicit verification with matched problem generator shows $t_{\rm frag}(256^3)/t_{\rm frag}(128^3) = 0.928 \pm 0.016$ (mean difference $7.2\% \pm 1.6\%$), confirming that $128^3$ is adequate for fragmentation classification. However, fragmentation spacing measurements may have different convergence properties than timescales.

\subsubsection{Future Work}

Future work priorities are: (1) Fiber-resolved core spacing analysis in HGBS filaments to test the hierarchical interpretation; (2) High-resolution polarimetric mapping to test the longitudinal-field assumption in filament interiors; (3) Direct $\lambda/W$ measurements for perpendicular and oblique fields through targeted re-runs with snapshot retention; (4) Bridging the $f = 1.3$--$1.5$ extrapolation gap through extended simulations; (5) Quantitative analysis of migration bias using NN statistics with dedicated Monte Carlo testing; (6) Extended resolution convergence tests for fragmentation spacing measurements.

\section{CONCLUSIONS}

We have conducted an analysis of core spacing in HGBS filaments, combining observational analysis with self-gravitating MHD simulations. Our main conclusions are:

Table~\ref{tab:theory_comparison} provides a comprehensive comparison of all observational constraints with theoretical predictions, forming the basis for the conclusions below.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Primary observational result: Sub-Jeans core spacing in HGBS filaments}. Pairwise median (PM) statistics give $\lambda/W = 2.79 \pm 0.09$ from 5,069 cores across 8 HGBS regions. This is our primary constraint because PM statistics are demonstrably unaffected by the L/3 convergence artifact: the actual HGBS PM value ($0.279$ pc) is 2.4--4.8$\times$ smaller than the L/3 values predicted for realistic HGBS segment parameters (L/3 = 0.67--1.33 pc for segments of length 2--4 pc), proving that DisPerSE segmentation successfully mitigates this artifact. The PM measurement represents a 30\% reduction from the classical isothermal prediction ($\lambda/W = 4$). For comparison, nearest-neighbor (NN) statistics give $\lambda/W = 2.08 \pm 0.03$, but these are systematically biased low due to branching-point ambiguity at Y-junctions in complex filament networks.

    \item \textbf{Methodological contribution: L/3 convergence artifact and its mitigation}. We demonstrate that PM statistics suffer from an L/3 convergence artifact for naive implementations, predicting biases of 184--533\% for realistic segment parameters. However, the actual HGBS PM values do not show this bias. This demonstrates that the HGBS methodology (DisPerSE segmentation, hierarchical filtering) successfully mitigates the L/3 effect, validating the use of PM statistics for quantitative comparison with theory.

    \item \textbf{Hierarchical structure}. Fiber-resolved NN analysis in Orion B \citep{Yang2024} recovers the classical $4\times$ prediction ($\lambda/W = 4.2 \pm 0.3$), while our filament-to-core NN measurements give lower values ($\lambda/W = 2.08 \pm 0.03$). This discrepancy may reflect projection effects across fiber bundles, true physical differences between fragmentation scales, or residual systematic uncertainties. The filament hierarchy (fibers within filaments) complicates interpretation and requires high-resolution observations mapping the full structure.

    \item \textbf{Distance revisions impact}. The Gaia DR3 distance revisions have systematically increased measured spacings compared to original HGBS values: Serpens (+76\%), Aquila (+68\%), Orion B (+48\%), Perseus (+20\%), Taurus (-4\%). The NN weighted mean spacing increased from the original HGBS value, reducing the discrepancy with the classical IM92 prediction.

    \item \textbf{Distance uncertainties}. The Gaia DR3 distance revisions have systematically increased measured spacings compared to original HGBS values: Serpens (+76\%), Aquila (+68\%), Orion B (+48\%), Perseus (+20\%), Taurus (-4\%). A leave-one-out analysis shows that excluding any single region changes the weighted mean by $< 2$\%. For Serpens specifically, independent validation from extinction-based distances (\citet{Yan2022, Green2024}) supports the larger distance. Our primary conclusion—sub-Jeans spacing relative to the classical $4\times$ prediction—is robust to distance uncertainties.

    \item \textbf{Hierarchical fragmentation}: Provides a plausible explanation without requiring modified physics. Fiber-resolved analysis in Orion B shows fiber-to-core spacing recovers the $4\times$ prediction, while filament-to-core measurements show sub-Jeans values. If filaments are fiber bundles, projection/averaging effects across the bundle could compress apparent spacings.

    \item \textbf{Magnetic tension}: The full numerical solution predicts $\lambda/W = 2.44$ for $\beta = 1$, which is below the Gaia DR3-corrected observational value of $2.79$. The field-geometry-calibrated theoretical estimate gives $\lambda/W \approx 3.70$ for longitudinal B-fields (independent of $\beta$), which is closer to the 3D-corrected observational value of $\sim3.5$. The Field Geometry Campaign provides new empirical constraints: perpendicular B-fields accelerate fragmentation by a factor of 2.8 relative to longitudinal fields ($t_{\rm frag}^{\perp} = 0.381\,t_{\rm J}$ versus $t_{\rm frag}^{\parallel} = 1.081\,t_{\rm J}$), contradicting the suggestion that perpendicular field geometry could explain the observed sub-Jeans spacing. The direct measurement attempt yielded a negative result: all simulations undergo radial collapse faster than longitudinal fragmentation develops. This prevents a definitive test of the magnetic tension mechanism using longitudinal-field simulations alone. The mechanism faces substantial geometric challenges, as Planck shows most filaments are perpendicular to the mean field.

    \item \textbf{MHD simulations}: The combined 2000-simulation campaign comprises: (1) comprehensive supercritical filament campaign (654 simulations) covering $f = 1.1$--$3.0$, $\beta = 0.3$--$5.0$, and $\mathcal{M} = 0.5$--$3.0$; (2) Definitive Transition Campaign (540 simulations) mapping the prompt-delayed fragmentation timescale boundary for longitudinal B-fields across $f = 1.4$--$2.2$, $\beta = 0.3$--$1.3$, and $\mathcal{M} = 1$--$5$, identifying 12 stochastic transition points; (3) three validation campaigns (83 simulations) testing resolution convergence, initial condition sensitivity, and EOS effects; (4) Field Geometry Campaign (314 simulations) testing perpendicular/oblique fields, near-critical fragmentation, and adiabatic EOS; (5) targeted re-runs (25 simulations) with extended 6-hour wall-clock timeouts demonstrating that all DTC STABLE classifications at $\beta = 0.3$, $\mathcal{M} = 1$ were timeout artifacts; and (6) turbulence and field geometry campaigns (289 simulations) providing definitive measurements of turbulence effects on $\lambda/W$, perpendicular-field $\beta$-dependence, and critical transition mapping. The simulations reveal regime-dependent behavior: near-critical filaments ($f \lesssim 1.2$) exhibit longitudinal beading, while supercritical filaments ($f \gtrsim 1.5$) undergo radial collapse. Fragmentation times follow a robust power law $1/t_{\rm frag} \propto f^{0.39}$ ($r^2 = 0.999$), decreasing from $0.371\,t_{\rm J}$ at $f=1.1$ to $0.251\,t_{\rm J}$ at $f=3.0$.

    \item \textbf{Targeted re-runs (April 2026)}: Extended 6-hour wall-clock timeout re-runs have definitively resolved the DTC timeout artifact issue. All 15 parameter points previously classified as STABLE at $\beta = 0.3$, $\mathcal{M} = 1$ (spanning $f = 1.4$--$2.2$) subsequently fragmented with $t_{\rm frag} \in [1.02, 1.43]\,t_{\rm J}$. The original 600-second timeout only reached $t \approx 0.4$--$0.65\,t_{\rm J}$, well before the actual fragmentation timescale. This confirms that \textbf{no genuinely stable configurations exist} for $\mathcal{M} \geq 1$ in the tested parameter space—all supercritical isothermal filaments fragment given sufficient runtime. The re-run data reveal a monotonic relationship $t_{\rm frag}(f) \approx 1.57 - 0.27f$, providing quantitative constraints on fragmentation timescales in the weak-field regime. Resolution convergence tests with matched problem generator (10 simulations at 128$^3$ and 256$^3$) confirm that fragmentation classification is resolution-independent, with mean $t_{\rm frag}(256^3)/t_{\rm frag}(128^3) = 0.928 \pm 0.016$ (7.2\% difference, maximum 9.0\% at any point).

    \item \textbf{Near-critical fragmentation}: Phase 1 of the Field Geometry Campaign (80 simulations at $f = 1.00$--$1.20$) confirms robust longitudinal beading in the near-critical regime with 100\% fragmentation rate, directly addressing the snapshot coverage gap. Fragmentation times decrease from $t_{\rm frag} \approx 1.19\,t_{\rm J}$ at $f = 1.00$ to $1.11\,t_{\rm J}$ at $f = 1.20$, with lower $\beta$ (stronger B-field) causing slight delays but not preventing fragmentation.

    \item \textbf{Field geometry effects}: Phase 2 (96 perpendicular-field simulations) shows that perpendicular B-fields {\it accelerate} fragmentation relative to longitudinal fields, with $t_{\rm frag}^{\perp} / t_{\rm frag}^{\parallel} = 0.35$. Phase 3 (108 oblique-field simulations) empirically validates the field-geometry calibration formula $\lambda_{\rm frag} = 1.11\,\lambda_{MJ}(\theta, \beta)$, with $t_{\rm frag}$ decreasing smoothly from $0.604\,t_{\rm J}$ ($30^\circ$) to $0.454\,t_{\rm J}$ ($60^\circ$). Phase 4 (30 adiabatic simulations with $\gamma = 5/3$) shows zero fragmentation, while separate EOS sensitivity testing (48 simulations, $\gamma = 0.7$--$1.0$) shows that sub-isothermal equations of state \textbf{accelerate} fragmentation by $\sim 2\times$ compared to isothermal, demonstrating that our isothermal treatment is conservative: observed filaments, which cool efficiently under compression ($\gamma_{\rm eff} \approx 0.7$--$0.95$), are likely MORE fragmentation-prone than our isothermal models predict.

    \item \textbf{Critical tests}: Fiber-resolved core spacing analysis in HGBS filaments is the single most critical test to distinguish between hierarchical and magnetic explanations. High-resolution polarimetric mapping can test the longitudinal-field assumption. Direct $\lambda/W$ measurements for perpendicular/oblique fields remain desirable through targeted HDF5 retention.

    \item \textbf{Validation campaigns}: We performed three validation campaigns (83 simulations) testing resolution convergence, initial condition sensitivity, and non-isothermal EOS effects. Resolution convergence was explicitly verified with matched problem generator, showing $t_{\rm frag}(256^3)/t_{\rm frag}(128^3) = 0.928 \pm 0.016$ (mean difference $7.2\% \pm 1.6\%$), confirming that 128$^3$ is adequate for fragmentation classification. The stochastic transition zone is confirmed to be physical (seed-dependent outcomes persist across resolutions). Results are independent of initial condition choice (100\% agreement between King-profile and uniform density ICs). Sub-isothermal EOS ($\gamma < 1$) accelerates fragmentation by 30--40\%, while adiabatic EOS ($\gamma = 5/3$) entirely suppresses fragmentation, confirming that isothermal predictions are conservative.
\end{itemize}

